Sleipnir demo: a team builds a small shop, against a cache-faithful mock endpoint (about twenty seconds).
Everything but the model is real: git worktrees, the merge queue and its checks, mail, the cache planner, the governor, accounting.

manager: Done. The shop has a catalogue with paging, a cart that totals in whole cents, the web handlers for both and smoke tests; every piece went through the merge queue and the checks pass on the result.

  agents             9 (1 manager, 8 workers)
  tasks              8 of 8 accepted by the harness after its own gate
  merge queue        4 merged, 1 sent back (each merge was checked on the merged result)
  mail               2 messages between agents
  model requests     55 in 20.662s
  input tokens       319152 read from cache, 0 written, 65816 uncached (hit ratio 83%)
  cache              2 compactions, 5 cache anomalies (the provider dropped its cache once, on purpose), 1 nudge to an agent that repeated itself
  cost               $0.0803
    if every agent kept a private cache   $0.0953 (16% less with the shared prefix)
    with no caching at all                $0.1999 (60% less)

Prices are round demo numbers ($0.50/M input, $0.125/M cached, $2.00/M output) and the cache lives 25s here, not minutes, so that you can watch it cool; the model is a script.
